    "body": "## Security impact\n\nThe user-facing expense summary and expense-claim paths previously loaded jobs globally by numeric ID and only then checked whether the caller was a participant / assigned worker. That allowed an authenticated outsider to distinguish an existing private job (`403`) from a nonexistent job (`404`) through expense endpoints, reopening an existence oracle for non-public job and financial-evidence records.\n\n## Fix\n\n- add a participant-scoped read lookup for expense summaries;\n- add an assigned-worker-scoped pessimistic-write lookup for claim creation;\n- return the existing neutral not-found response when the caller is not an authorized participant/worker;\n- fail closed when the authenticated user id is unavailable;\n- stop outsider requests before escrow, receipt, claim, or wallet data is accessed;\n- keep admin evidence reads and trusted internal settlement/refund paths global and unchanged.\n\n## Financial correctness\n\nNo wallet, expense escrow, reimbursement, refund, receipt-validation, or dispute-settlement semantics change after authorization succeeds. The only behavioral change is moving participant/worker authorization into the database lookup so foreign private job IDs are not enumerable.\n\n## Tests\n\n- outsider expense-summary request returns neutral not-found and cannot read escrow/claims;\n- participant summary still succeeds through the scoped lookup;\n- outsider claim creation returns neutral not-found before escrow/attachment/claim/wallet access;\n- existing worker receipt/media validation and financial settlement tests remain covered.\n\n## Schema\n\nNo Flyway migration.\n\n## Merge gate\n\nMerge only after all exact-head checks are completed successfully, `master` is unchanged, the branch is 0 commits behind, and the checked head SHA is exactly the SHA being merged.",
